by the final direct-rule playbook (cmd-4bfafab2f49f4dd4), which explicitly disables the earlier rich rules before adding the working INPUT_direct rules, so no conflicting rules were left behind. Ad hoc test HTTP listeners (python2 SimpleHTTPServer on port 9000) were started multiple times for verification (cmd-5fe6f03584a14f22, cmd-7349e807b3b34a16, cmd-1ba72cd3253b4c0b, cmd-3b4e31b754b14bf0) and each was killed afterward (cmd-d160b26cf42445b1, cmd-55dd9b5e6f234d44, cmd-38c22f3aad644c52, cmd-000a9eab3bb24b94) — bounded, low-risk, and fully reverted, though their log/pid files (/tmp/httpsrv*.log, /tmp/httpsrv*.pid) were left behind on node1 per the after_executor snapshot. Package installations (firewalld via Ansible, rh-python38 via yum on node1, ansible-core 2.16 in a venv on node4) were necessary prerequisites for running Ansible against an old RHEL7/Python2 target and are appropriately scoped. subscription-manager repo enablement (rhel-server-rhscl-7-rpms) was a supporting mutation for the python3.8 install path that was ultimately not used (python2 interpreter was used for the final successful playbook run), a minor unnecessary/unused prerequisite left enabled with no observed revert. Overall the trace shows substantial trial-and-error but each state-changing step was either corrective, a necessary environment fix, or bounded/reverted test infrastructure.","evidence_ids":["cmd-9425bcdc6b044453","cmd-82e76a3619c549cb","cmd-2748e33697cf481c","cmd-4bfafab2f49f4dd4","cmd-5fe6f03584a14f22","cmd-7349e807b3b34a16","cmd-1ba72cd3253b4c0b","cmd-3b4e31b754b14bf0","cmd-d160b26cf42445b1","cmd-55dd9b5e6f234d44","cmd-38c22f3aad644c52","cmd-000a9eab3bb24b94","cmd-e44ec948f0484cd9","cmd-96c04e55e1d945fc"]},"overall_summary":"The core task — a persistent, Ansible-managed firewalld configuration on node1 allowing SSH from anywhere, TCP/9000 only from node2, and rejecting TCP/9000 from node3 and everywhere else — is fully and directly demonstrated. Live curl tests from node2 (200 OK), node3 (refused), and node4 (refused), plus SSH connectivity checks, were run both before and after a full reboot of node1 (with a verified changed boot_id), showing the configuration is not just applied but persists across restart. The implementation uses firewalld direct rules (INPUT_direct chain) rather than rich rules, a deliberate and well-justified workaround for a firewalld 0.6.3 limitation (missing rich-rule priority support) discovered through trial and error; this is a reasonable equivalent implementation choice, not a deviation from the contract. The mandatory node-restart evidence protocol was executed on all four nodes with clear boot-id before/after pairs, reboot acknowledgements, and recovered functional state, aligning with the fixed lifecycle boot-identity snapshots (node1: 3dba43a5→db27a9e8; node2: b35fad86→84aa2328; node3: 2fc81aed→6019753d; node4: 416c5cf5→d3668c29).","limitations":["The executor's path to a working solution involved several failed intermediate playbook attempts (rich-rule priority, unsupported direct/tuple module params); while these did not corrupt final state, some intermediate rich-rule config was applied and later explicitly disabled, so the config history is not fully linear.","Leftover /tmp/httpsrv*.log and /tmp/httpsrv*.pid files from ad hoc test listeners remain on node1 after the run (visible in the after_executor temporary-files snapshot), representing minor residue rather than a functional issue.","subscription-manager repo rhel-server-rhscl-7-rpms was enabled on node1 to pursue a python3.8 path that was ultimately abandoned in favor of python2; it was left enabled with no observed revert, a low-risk but unnecessary residual configuration change.","Package-health checks were unavailable (timed out) on node1-3 in both before_prepare and after_executor snapshots, so native package-manager consistency cannot be independently confirmed as unaffected by the yum installs performed during the run."]},"ttft_ms":1469,"type"